On Thu, 20 Sep 2018 at 18:58, Scott Devine <smde...@ucdavis.edu> wrote:

> Yes, these are all good pointers, but if you are using non-rectangular shapefile polygons as part of the process, here's something else to be aware of:
>
> Mask will return a raster with the same extent as the original raster but with elevations converted to NA outside the shapefile polygon.
>
> Crop will return a raster with an extent that the shapefile can fit into.  So, except for rectangular or square polygons used for cropping, you will still have elevations in your "cropped" raster that are actually outside of the polygon boundary.
>
> I'm not sure what your end goal is, but it's possible you may want to do a crop (to reduce the extent and memory demands of the raster) followed by a mask (to have data only for your area of interest).
>
> Scott
>
>

> On Thu, Sep 20, 2018 at 9:38 AM, Matthieu Stigler <mmst...@ucdavis.edu> wrote:

> > Hi
> >
> > Yes, with extract, you are returning a list (or data.frame with df=TRUE). But note that extract is meant usually to do shape-wise statistics (compute mean for every country, etc). I suspect in your case what you mean by "extract" is covered by the crop() function: https://www.rdocumentation.org/packages/raster/versions/2.6-7/topics/crop
> >
> > Note that the crop function can write directly the raster! This should save you a lot of effort (and lot of on-cache disk writing). The ... argument in crop() refer to arguments in writeRaster directly!
> >
> > Best,
> >
> > Matthieu

> > On Thu, Sep 20, 2018 at 6:54 AM Steve Fick <stephe...@gmail.com> wrote:

> > > Hey Ozias,
> > >
> > > Raster::extract returns a list when given a spatialPolygons object (see 'value' section in https://www.rdocumentation.org/packages/raster/versions/2.6-7/topics/extract). I'd run through the code without looping first to see if that is the output you want. Otherwise you may be looking for the raster::mask function instead.
> > >
> > > Also the R-Sig-geo listserve is a good way to get spatial questions answered quickly.
> > >
> > > Best,
> > >
